# Everything Is a Component

Structure all UI as **named, exportable components**. Pages and layouts should compose components only; avoid large inline JSX. This keeps the codebase ready for future Builder.io (non-coders editing via a visual builder).

## Rules

- **Pages** (`app/**/*.tsx`): Import and compose components only. No big blocks of JSX in the page file.
- **Sections and blocks**: Each distinct section (hero, features, footer, CTA, etc.) is its own component in `components/` or a feature folder.
- **Reusable pieces**: Buttons, cards, headings, layouts → components. No one-off inline markup that could be a component.
- **Naming**: Use clear, semantic names (e.g. `HeroSection`, `ProductGrid`, `CheckoutForm`).

## Examples

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — page full of inline JSX
export default function Page() {
  return (
    <main>
      <header><h1>...</h1><nav>...</nav></header>
      <section><h2>...</h2><p>...</p></section>
      <section><div className="grid">...</div></section>
    </main>
  );
}

// ✅ GOOD — page composes components
export default function Page() {
  return (
    <main>
      <SiteHeader />
      <HeroSection />
      <FeaturesGrid />
    </main>
  );
}
```

- New UI = new component (or reuse existing). Extract as soon as a block is identifiable (section, card, form, list).
- Keep components in `components/` (or domain folders) with one primary component per file unless tightly related (e.g. component + subcomponents in same file).
